following statements are true?\n\n- $y=1$ is a source.\n- $y=-2$ is a sink.\n- $y=-2$ is a node." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/f82ff6ec76ab3cf26d2da516b6bf5c5d9773824043a38a26c8db8a16150bfc86.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=1$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339711/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339711"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-98763"] :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+6y+9 & =0 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=-3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $(y+3)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/074ed04c41f20a891678d61a26ae06f1151299110dfd13d2c8b7f6eef3e6e363.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=-3$ is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-3$ is a node\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-98762/selection/1aab109a7de1d550805b8f22b3c4edfc453c59c68fb54b4dd3932c3e4eb1a58f" :answer/id #uuid "2ac9fc4e-c55c-5f62-b3e5-7f438f2a2922" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a node" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98762/selection/649f5b7236d39820de0720e619a1f1cbf8776f16623b927b6b469bb1ec8fb938" :answer/id #uuid "609e3f91-a0a8-5b98-b6bd-b04e9fb74760" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98762/selection/4cf51581a2cbb8b98521cbdb64164a531ea2016e378142ed828f94a2f830990d" :answer/id #uuid "bbee1050-db00-5617-9d18-842b1d40cce3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-1$ and $y=2$ are both sinks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98762/selection/6e290a556cdfad84dc04c002f7b3a96224175aac2bd090ccfc895aa241f42007" :answer/id #uuid "3a7ca8a5-0e83-5f5c-8e2a-bf764acf3c01" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-1$ is a sink, and $y=2$ is a source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98762/selection/6d1f2881f0291e9a104618947d8b5281a6fcf7df71670acf40ce3a134dd8c44d" :answer/id #uuid "e87ddba3-fdd2-593f-9d17-72b4c80ffd0e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-1$ and $y=2$ are both sources" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98762/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "88fb2801-d362-51d7-9bac-cb9f73be7aa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98762/selection/649f5b7236d39820de0720e619a1f1cbf8776f16623b927b6b469bb1ec8fb938" :answer-field/choices ["q-98762/selection/1aab109a7de1d550805b8f22b3c4edfc453c59c68fb54b4dd3932c3e4eb1a58f" "q-98762/selection/649f5b7236d39820de0720e619a1f1cbf8776f16623b927b6b469bb1ec8fb938" "q-98762/selection/4cf51581a2cbb8b98521cbdb64164a531ea2016e378142ed828f94a2f830990d" "q-98762/selection/6e290a556cdfad84dc04c002f7b3a96224175aac2bd090ccfc895aa241f42007" "q-98762/selection/6d1f2881f0291e9a104618947d8b5281a6fcf7df71670acf40ce3a134dd8c44d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98762" :question/id #uuid "26ed5480-33c8-5f24-be06-ea5c8236ac1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-98762" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=2+y-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ 2+y-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ -({y}^{2}-y-2) & =0 \\\\ -(y+1)(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y+1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and positive for $y\\in (-1,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/9230d847daa518edfaad43f6552a48c2b3cac9523a1e9ecad8624851080e00ea.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-1$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\n- $y=2$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-1$ is a source, and $y=2$ is a sink\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-98762/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98762"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-98760"] :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)({y}^{2}+6y+9) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)(y+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-3,3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-3$ and $y=3.$\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(y+3)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3),$ and negative for $y\\in (3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/3c118c900486d8561864c88e1b25a461c8f459f810bb7b753d30d9d0d4780913.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-3$  is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-340352/selection/8270465cacddc7144bccc14a6aad7f15b6206e9f539ecbb9c050e1ed3132dce7" :answer/id #uuid "ab29185a-3f7d-5687-9b73-c87bf119d86d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-4$ is a source, and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340352/selection/82a11a934fa571328f5b79ffc7ef299afc203553399a55dd58ee97105a50ca9d" :answer/id #uuid "e0b012bc-8824-524f-83d7-793026b25963" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a node" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340352/selection/3f3e004e49562a07ac62cc4c7d74dc5d0ee86b4adc70e6232eb2fed01bd56a7c" :answer/id #uuid "3b1ad301-b613-5f37-abdc-b31222f32b1a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-4$ and $y=3$ are both nodes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340352/selection/78e51941bf81a88172bc5fb1785c8f425e7cdadf26d54e06e90b9d801aa5a69b" :answer/id #uuid "85d4ae47-dfc9-57c5-94cc-5b62373f1e96" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-4$ and $y=3$ are both sinks" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340352/selection/609b9132e2850a4f3a5872f7eabd57d2835d89922a1d5f06227b2a5054ea854a" :answer/id #uuid "7fbf500b-2d85-50cf-873f-c5be838b86f2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-340352/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b248ce1-1d54-5682-92d0-3e8187a3f079" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-340352/selection/609b9132e2850a4f3a5872f7eabd57d2835d89922a1d5f06227b2a5054ea854a" :answer-field/choices ["q-340352/selection/8270465cacddc7144bccc14a6aad7f15b6206e9f539ecbb9c050e1ed3132dce7" "q-340352/selection/82a11a934fa571328f5b79ffc7ef299afc203553399a55dd58ee97105a50ca9d" "q-340352/selection/3f3e004e49562a07ac62cc4c7d74dc5d0ee86b4adc70e6232eb2fed01bd56a7c" "q-340352/selection/78e51941bf81a88172bc5fb1785c8f425e7cdadf26d54e06e90b9d801aa5a69b" "q-340352/selection/609b9132e2850a4f3a5872f7eabd57d2835d89922a1d5f06227b2a5054ea854a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-340352" :question/id #uuid "e59e11c9-19f2-5fd2-8af6-91ba29833c0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-340352" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}+y-12?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+y-12 & =0 \\\\ (y+4)(y-3) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-4$ and $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-4)\\cup (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-4,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/c2bb2cbc11afb9bb2b25fb2105d19c2748541592c77bcecab342db69834b4fe2.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-4$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-4$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-340352/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-340352"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98764/selection/5ed25ea65d0350a3b8db02f5f7739b54651c2ab820e13cb4661377dd0db7a5a6" :answer/id #uuid "a3461d9d-84d3-53b6-b0d4-708719f99a06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a sink and $y=2$ is a source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98764/selection/e63c4a1708e1e7856cd2ccb02c79b22bd3516eece578f0e78329726820c282df" :answer/id #uuid "b63fcaaf-89e8-5a4f-9093-3e9c557b7860" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a source and $y=2$ is a sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98764/selection/ffefc57b7bc9bfbed3722daf05ef423b34fbf2d7ae0fb065ae3fc30722f13d6f" :answer/id #uuid "9cea031e-d5f4-523c-a0a2-20858738ac67" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98764/selection/1fac82389c0c977182326b80c86861e612a760d1604f3cd38838f95b52a876c6" :answer/id #uuid "5efec162-dfd7-5d56-8603-5427baed3bcf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98764/selection/e22c13de591e8efc12b2f437c004bfc211d741ddae96a5638540ce39970aac17" :answer/id #uuid "d06eae3a-ee13-5a52-9291-9ad56da88bbf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$y=-2$ is a node" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98764/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8cb6e6b4-dbda-5053-981d-78e441cb259b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98764/selection/1fac82389c0c977182326b80c86861e612a760d1604f3cd38838f95b52a876c6" :answer-field/choices ["q-98764/selection/5ed25ea65d0350a3b8db02f5f7739b54651c2ab820e13cb4661377dd0db7a5a6" "q-98764/selection/e63c4a1708e1e7856cd2ccb02c79b22bd3516eece578f0e78329726820c282df" "q-98764/selection/ffefc57b7bc9bfbed3722daf05ef423b34fbf2d7ae0fb065ae3fc30722f13d6f" "q-98764/selection/1fac82389c0c977182326b80c86861e612a760d1604f3cd38838f95b52a876c6" "q-98764/selection/e22c13de591e8efc12b2f437c004bfc211d741ddae96a5638540ce39970aac17"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98764" :question/id #uuid "8d8bc14b-d6cd-5708-b7ce-c5b5f753bf7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98764" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y+2)({y}^{2}+2)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y+2)({y}^{2}+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=-2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}+2$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (2,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/ac2d3df6e9caa672e16f3499502bba9e17c5d79dd200055319c22bc29fb78340.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-2$ is a source\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-98764/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98764"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-98774"] :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-4$ (already drawn by the above property) and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/e5ba765b9caffab904f07c5086d3206f72e36e66cb018673ce7ed44d79d19e88.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=-4$ appear to converge toward this line." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-98809/selection/fa463d4be1485c0a88790fde32a4cedd2674f08488b77d5b27331bffe910c2f9" :answer/id #uuid "9cf34c76-9a6b-55f7-91c3-b3b90e308e8c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98809/selection/2a3bc485198aa558ff238d98464a850764216b379f1b7c17fa238734e985d6d2" :answer/id #uuid "228ba247-55c9-59c7-b24c-8437fe676fd5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/a7961c00b93091e3bf775f6f61f503d9f3d16ad30174c4f3310654f5aa75721c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98809/selection/50da65e19ca2681d543db4fe0a9e697b25a71d59b8a1624fc847ba4b9084a6df" :answer/id #uuid "0a059886-3fef-54e8-b1a1-ee91db98426b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/f1302588da5388ebfa996005675625f98a9edee7c5ec0e99a2c897e70bd7e680.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98809/selection/faf7a6573d7c21a99f010cbb0c7ab80283ffc260d25b0821b06298391fb41d58" :answer/id #uuid "12bfde5c-5dff-5290-aa13-8a8176435aef" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/4e360e24357a714978fdfb64b239381b3d7c66d13a1307c265f067881aaebc54.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98809/selection/c95c467d6481f6134f752366fcf103634319411849bc792b74c6f42e9b859458" :answer/id #uuid "02d24914-7b96-52c2-af1b-ebe93891dde5" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/7df58db225ecb32e6b51b2c16d78a557ca57e63f5d85551dfc1ddc85865e9ea4.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98809/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "066a9351-b2a2-5970-8d1f-ebf0bdbaa5a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-98809/selection/fa463d4be1485c0a88790fde32a4cedd2674f08488b77d5b27331bffe910c2f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-98809/selection/fa463d4be1485c0a88790fde32a4cedd2674f08488b77d5b27331bffe910c2f9" "q-98809/selection/2a3bc485198aa558ff238d98464a850764216b379f1b7c17fa238734e985d6d2" "q-98809/selection/50da65e19ca2681d543db4fe0a9e697b25a71d59b8a1624fc847ba4b9084a6df" "q-98809/selection/faf7a6573d7c21a99f010cbb0c7ab80283ffc260d25b0821b06298391fb41d58" "q-98809/selection/c95c467d6481f6134f752366fcf103634319411849bc792b74c6f42e9b859458"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-98809" :question/id #uuid "a90c5eac-a818-5712-a0c3-9443f22bd769" :question/math-academy-id "q-98809" :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=-2$ is a node\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=-2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just above $y=-2$ points upward, the arrow just below it points upward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/5ea8af3b30df51189c3511dbe093f05b309dc50cbdea1e52d5ba3a42d88101c2.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/5737491c0a472a12cb3fd9e0b09cb5ede88b193a3f084ca89671e4669b945d5b.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-98809/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98809"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-98790/selection/7fbd7253f77ae12dcd5a049451ad2b00733c113ff5ab838696d325325aa4a8eb" :answer/id #uuid "43bf8b08-d2cc-5db0-9bb0-8174adeb27c0" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/ad11e5508156e7b17e0c3ff743af19fb4d25ac0746315687cc45ee4bcca46960.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98790/selection/2661a949ff54cb4d90347bc268bb3f24a56023e94ecffa272fbcc75f509d6c68" :answer/id #uuid "54c1869d-478b-587d-b16e-4d2d7d5b8c4b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/8289068f50fdec4729cf2a2c402040b3ec162d2769e27cd7605a6b834e513a4c.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98790/selection/c13729704b601faea3a5acb117bcb8508ff6fc56319531a47a88e10b8b112774" :answer/id #uuid "081711a9-40b2-57f2-a704-90fbccc54d58" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/a519627a8061e39559b35506ef44ef44ee32c01aa32d8d21157261f14cb94bd4.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98790/selection/32e30a4f683341b8532234ec3d929d6daaeb269b2de6390c9f5393ab8776f395" :answer/id #uuid "4f9dee5a-0605-52b0-bdd2-d2adc8422aa7" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/83e01284a8016cecf4cc0262c83269948bc089c5b890c6122909dc39c730dd9b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-98790/selection/cf2676790e23d243341363534daa4a743132d3926ab589512a5da93e94f93c64" :answer/id #uuid "255f3643-a7ca-5f16-a68b-cb221d6ec4c6" :answer/type :answer.type/