e're given that $y=-3$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-3$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=2$ points upward, the arrow just above it points upward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/d03ab1d15be3656787d7ccd0114b2011f561bfed9c67d22f3366f50ed2f410e4.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/890c699885c8694597912bd65044fde50dd99ac8757578dc0db71bdb413b5db7.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-3$ and $y=2.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-3$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-98790/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58b04cc-da5e-5c51-a583-0663d783c697"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-98790"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-96557"] :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=2$ is a node\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=2$ is a node. This means that the arrows on either side of $y=2$ point in the same direction. Since the arrow just below $y=2$ points upward, the arrow just above it points upward too.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/70c6b5e466eb64695ec099bba5ee02fbacf89c60b0eac48ebdb4dbe47735bf14.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/f3789101292914032885458730bfaabf42e75a9447f43a1e7409c15fb8cbbaef.png)\n\n \n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=2$ appear to diverge away from this line when we're looking above the line and appear to converge toward the line when we're looking below the line." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-98811"] :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $3$ equilibrium points, $y=-2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=2.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n- We're given that $y=2$ is also a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/b5afe944331145c5253b2f1100f642b8a91a538295f3c84877eeb86870a04915.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/fb27ad885fc87fa56a04ec92d46173c98fda170f11117c4748c21a67faa9e166.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2,$ $y=0,$ and $y=2.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to converge toward this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=2$ appear to diverge away from this line." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8650"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/f495896e6df702a60eea6825d7d72c31e978d92d68ec7b1779fe565a8dc9b946.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=1$ is a source\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink\n\n- $y=-2$ is a node" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8538"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/87dbc31b08ad8735b7a8e9f18bc74c448a221fe665acca3d40208786f20459ce.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y= -3$ is a sink.\n\n- $y= -1$ is a node.\n\n- $y= 2$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/413ddd3248fb53bbd623794970ee2b4a561d17e2659bb72cf864436d9fffa792.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-3$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8536"] :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}-2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}(y-2) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=0$ and $y=2.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y^2$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $y-2$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n| $f(y)$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{red}-}$ | ${\\color{blue}+}$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (2,\\infty),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty,0) \\cup (0,2).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/420aaceb6f78b8a6aa55f8e0a2a9a87e604875180a9321ff34bd72ca2384ec09.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=0$ is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=2$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=0$ is a node, and $y=2$ is a source\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8537"] :question/problem "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y= -2$ is a source\n\n- $y= 3$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/07fcd4a3db9ed24d7e0c391095e5950c11be4b5dac2dcc5b4cda229d69f2aaa4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE $y' = f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- We're given that $y=-2$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=-2$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=3$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=3$ and an upward-pointing arrow just below it (already drawn by the above property).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/0af6403f55ca8193993fee474183676ad4edfb0b99b4bf4707993ff512700358.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071359/assets/3faccf2319817824a473b127ee7eff831ae7de82d9198b541b4d89dd7ef58ec2.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=-2$ and $y=3.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=-2$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=3$ appear to converge toward this line." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
